# Label-space conventions

Every figure in this report carries a one-line **figure tag** in its
footer, e.g.

```
L=SP5/MID-     | flare=>name | rye=>name
L=SP5/MID→eur  | flare=>name | rf=>name
```

The tag is the complete answer to "what label space is this graph in
and how did each tool reach it?" Two charts with identical tags are
directly comparable; two charts with different tags are not — even
when they share axis labels. The tag lists **only the tools that
actually participate in the chart**.

## The label spaces

+-----------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+
| Space           | Members (ordered)              | Notes                                            |
+:================+:===============================+:=================================================+
| `SP6`           | afr, amr, eas, eur, mid, sas   | The six-way continental superpopulation set.     |
+-----------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+
| `SP5`           | afr, amr, eas, eur, sas        | `SP6` with MID removed.                          |
+-----------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+
| `SP6.sub`       | afr.1, afr.2, eur.1, …         | A subcontinental refinement of `SP6`, used when  |
|                 |                                | one tool's components fold into a single         |
|                 |                                | continental label.                               |
+-----------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+
| `<tool>.native` | tool-defined                   | Each tool's native columns (e.g. FLARE's panel   |
|                 |                                | populations, Rye's Q columns).                   |
+-----------------+--------------------------------+--------------------------------------------------+

The shared space is named **SP** (superpopulation) — deliberately
not after any single tool.

**Subcomponent suffixes.** When a tool has more inferred components
than the target has labels, the extras share a label and pick up
**rank** suffixes: `afr.1` is the component most strongly correlated
with AFR; `afr.2` the next; and so on. The rank is by descending
correlation, so `afr.1` refers to the same biological signal across
runs as long as the reference is fixed.

## MID handling

When the two tools compared in a section share the same MID stance
(both have it, neither has it), the tag reads `MID+` or `MID-`. When
one tool carries MID and the other doesn't, the section commits to a
**rule** that brings them into a common SP5:

- `MID-` (drop) — MID mass on the MID-bearing side is dropped before
  comparison. Use when MID is genuinely absent from the cohort.
- `MID→eur` (fold) — MID mass on the MID-bearing side is added to
  the EUR column before MID is dropped. Use when one tool's reference
  panel lacks MID and we don't want to penalise it for absent MID
  signal that another tool reported as MID-flavoured EUR.

The rule appears in the tag for every figure where it matters.

## Matching methods

Each clause `<tool>=><method>` names the matching method that mapped
that tool's columns into the target space. Choose by what kind of
information the tool emits:

### `name` — exact-name match

Use when the tool already exposes label names (a VCF `##ANCESTRY=`
header, a TSV column header). Case-insensitive lookup; deterministic;
no algorithm. The cheapest and most defensible choice.

### `corrH` — correlation + Hungarian assignment

Use when both sides emit **populations** (allele-frequency vectors
per ancestry). Build the Pearson correlation matrix between the two
sides' rows; solve the optimal one-to-one matching by the Hungarian
algorithm.

A matching is **bijective** — a one-to-one pairing — when every
inferred component gets exactly one target label and every target
label gets exactly one inferred component. This is only possible
when there are at most as many inferred components as target labels
(`K_inf ≤ K_ref`):

```
K_inf = 6, K_ref = 6  ──► bijective pairing exists. Hungarian finds
                          the optimal one:
                          afr ←→ flare_3
                          amr ←→ flare_1
                          eas ←→ flare_5
                          eur ←→ flare_0
                          mid ←→ flare_4
                          sas ←→ flare_2

K_inf = 9, K_ref = 6  ──► pigeonhole. Cannot be bijective. Some
                          target labels receive multiple inferred
                          components → the "merge" branch. Each
                          extra component folds into its
                          best-correlated label, producing afr.1,
                          afr.2, ... subcomponents.
```

So `corrH` is bijective whenever `K_inf ≤ K_ref`. Cardinality is
forced by the K-shapes, not a knob.

### `postS` — posterior correlation + calibration-slope override

Use when one side is **per-sample posteriors** (a soft probability
vector per sample) and the other is a reference probability vector.
Each component picks the reference label it correlates best with
across samples. A second calibration-slope check catches
anti-correlated cases: a component that looks like AFR by
correlation but whose calibration curve has the wrong sign gets
reassigned to whatever label has a positive slope. Many-to-one (no
bijectivity guarantee).

### `confH` — Hungarian on a hard-call confusion matrix

Use when both sides emit **hard calls** at the same units (sites or
base pairs) and you want a one-to-one mapping. Count agreements per
(src_label, ref_label) pair; run Hungarian on the agreement matrix.
Bijective by construction when both sides have the same number of
labels; pads with dummies otherwise.

### `manual` — analyst-supplied CSV

Escape hatch when none of the above produce a defensible mapping.
The analyst commits a `component,label` CSV; the report tag carries
the file's hash so the mapping is reproducible.
